    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Strong Baseline, but **Negotiate**\nThis is a solid initial offer for a Google L6 Senior Product Manager, but **you should definitely negotiate**. \n\nYou hold maximum leverage: Google reached out to you, you were not actively looking, and you are happy and well-compensated at Meta. You should never leave a stable, well-paying role at Meta for an initial offer that takes a base pay cut and provides zero sign-on bonus.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Breakdown: Google vs. Meta\n\n#### The Vesting Schedule Factor (33/33/22/12)\nGoogle\u2019s standard new-hire GSU schedule is front-loaded:\n* **Year 1 (33%):** ~$356,400\n* **Year 2 (33%):** ~$356,400\n* **Year 3 (22%):** ~$237,600\n* **Year 4 (12%):** ~$129,600\n\n#### Year-by-Year Total Compensation Comparison\n\n| Year | Meta (Current Run Rate) | Google (Offered Initial Grant) | Difference (Pre-Refresh) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | ~$526,000 | **~$638,400** ($235k base + $47k bonus + $356.4k stock) | **+$112,400** |\n| **Year 2** | ~$526,000 | **~$638,400** ($235k base + $47k bonus + $356.4k stock) | **+$112,400** |\n| **Year 3** | ~$526,000 | **~$519,600** (+ whatever refreshes you earn) | -$6,400 (pre-refresh) |\n| **Year 4** | ~$526,000 | **~$411,600** (+ whatever refreshes you earn) | -$114,400 (pre-refresh) |\n\n* **The Good:** In Years 1 and 2, Google represents an immediate, meaningful step up of over $110k/year in compensation.\n* **The Reality Check:** The recruiter\u2019s point about refreshers is true, but refreshers depend heavily on performance ratings (GRAD) and take time to scale up. In Years 3 and 4, without strong refreshers, your initial grant drops off sharply.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Gaps in the Offer\n\n1. **Base Salary Pay Cut:** Your base drops from $240,000 to $235,000. The posted salary band for this role tops out at **$271,000**, meaning there is substantial headroom.\n2. **Missing Sign-On Bonus:** Google routinely uses sign-on bonuses ($50,000\u2013$100,000+) for L6 hires to offset unvested equity candidates leave on the table and bridge bonus payout cycles.\n3. **Leaving $200k Unvested at Meta:** Moving requires you to forfeit unvested stock and potentially your upcoming Meta bonus payout depending on timing.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Exactly to Ask For\n\nCounter with specific figures across three components:\n\n| Component | Current Offer | What to Counter With | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Closes the pay cut versus Meta and stays within the top quartile of their $183k\u2013$271k band. |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets leaving ~$200k in unvested Meta RSUs and timing differences in annual bonus vesting. |\n| **RSUs (4-year)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000** | Brings the annual average equity from $270k/yr to $312.5k/yr, softening the Year 3/4 cliff. |\n\n---\n\n### 5. How to Frame the Negotiation\n\nReach out to the recruiter via email or schedule a quick call using this positioning:\n\n> *\"Thank you for the offer. I enjoyed meeting the team and I'm genuinely excited about the scope of this L6 role.*\n> \n> *Because I wasn't looking to move and am in a great spot at Meta, compensation is a primary factor in my decision. Looking at the numbers, the base salary is a step down from my current $240k base, and walking away from Meta means leaving behind about $200k in equity vesting over the next 12 months as well as my upcoming bonus cycle.*\n> \n> *To make this an easy decision for me to sign, here is what would get me to accept:*\n> - *A base salary of **$255,000** (to reflect my current level and market rate)*\n> - *An equity grant of **$1,250,000** over 4 years*\n> - *A sign-on bonus of **$80,000** to help offset the unvested equity and bonus I'm walking away from.*\n> \n> *If we can get the package to this level, I am ready to sign.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Summary Checklist\n* **Do not accept as-is:** It is a respectable initial anchor, but they expect an L6 PM from Meta to counter.\n* **Do not walk away yet:** Google has clear budget flexibility in their L6 band for base, equity, and sign-on.\n* **Negotiate firmly:** Lead with your unvested equity at Meta and the base salary discrepancy to justify your numbers.",
