    "message": {
     "content": "### The Bottom Line: **Yes, you should accept this offer** (unless you have strong non-financial reasons to stay at Meta).\n\nFinancially, Google responded directly to your objection. Instead of giving you a cash sign-on, they rolled the entire $200,000 into your equity grant ($1,080,000 $\\rightarrow$ **$1,280,000**) and matched your Meta base salary at **$240,000**.\n\nBecause of Google\u2019s front-loaded vesting schedule, this puts you in an exceptionally strong financial position.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Updated Math\n\nWith Google\u2019s standard **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** vesting schedule, your compensation looks like this:\n\n* **Year 1 (33% equity):** $240,000 base + $48,000 bonus + $422,400 stock = **$710,400**\n* **Year 2 (33% equity):** $240,000 base + $48,000 bonus + $422,400 stock = **$710,400**\n* **Year 3 (22% equity):** $240,000 base + $48,000 bonus + $281,600 stock = **$569,600** *(pre-refreshes)*\n* **Year 4 (12% equity):** $240,000 base + $48,000 bonus + $153,600 stock = **$441,600** *(pre-refreshes)*\n\n#### Comparison to Meta ($526,000 / year run rate):\n\n| Period | Meta Run Rate | Google Revised Offer | Net Difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | ~$526,000 | **~$710,400** | **+$184,400** |\n| **Year 2** | ~$526,000 | **~$710,400** | **+$184,400** |\n| **2-Year Total** | **~$1,052,000** | **~$1,420,800** | **+$368,800** |\n\n#### What about the $200,000 left on the table?\nIn Year 1 alone at Google, your equity vest is **$422,400** (compared to the $200,000 you would have received at Meta). Over your first two years, you will make nearly **$370,000 more** than staying at Meta, fully offsetting what you leave behind.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Is There Any Room Left to Negotiate?\n**No.** When Google increases an L6 PM equity grant to **$1.28M** and states it is their best and final offer, it almost certainly is. \n\n* A 4-year annualized average of **~$608,000/year** is at the top band for Google L6 Senior PMs in the Bay Area.\n* Pushing further without a competing written offer from another company risks souring the relationship with the hiring manager before you start.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Non-Financial Factors to Weigh Before Signing\n\nSince the compensation clearly favors Google, your decision comes down to qualitative factors:\n\n1. **Scope & Team:** Is the Google product area high-priority and well-resourced?\n2. **Culture & Velocity:** Meta is famously bottom-up, fast-moving, and impact-driven. Google PM culture can involve significantly more stakeholder consensus, slower launch cycles, and matrixed approvals.\n3. **Career Trajectory (L6 $\\rightarrow$ L7):** Promoting from IC6 to IC7 at Meta versus L6 to L7 at Google. L7 PM at Google is notoriously difficult to achieve. If you are already close to an IC7 promotion at Meta, factor that in.\n\n---\n\n### Final Recommendation\n**Sign the offer.** An initial package guaranteeing over **$710,000/year** for your first two years is an exceptional outcome for an L6 lateral move.",
